- What changes if array length is not multiple of four?

## Why This Is Asked
This question tests practical memory management, lock-free data structures, and safe object reuse under contention. A beginner-friendly allocator emphasizes alignment and simple reclaim strategies.

## Key Concepts
- Lock-free freelist using CAS
- ABA avoidance (tagged pointers or hazard pointers)
- Slab-style pool with 64-byte blocks
- Cache-line awareness and fragmentation

## Code Example
```c
// Pseudo-code for allocate/free
typedef struct Block { struct Block* next; } Block;
typedef struct Pool { Block* head; void* pool; size_t block_size; } Pool;

Block* allocate(Pool* p){
  Block* old = atomic_load(&p->head);
  while (old){
    Block* next = old->next;
    if (atomic_compare_exchange_weak(&p->head, &old, next)){
      return old;
    }
  }
  return NULL;
}
void deallocate(Pool* p, Block* b){
  Block* head = atomic_load(&p->head);
  do {
    b->next = head;
  } while (!atomic_compare_exchange_weak(&p->head, &head, b));
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you detect pool exhaustion and recover?
- How would you extend to variable-size objects or per-object headers?

## Why This Is Asked

This question checks practical low-level memory management fundamentals in a multithreaded context.

## Key Concepts

- Thread-local freelists and embedded per-block next pointers
- 64-byte alignment and arena initialization
- Contention avoidance without global locks and basic fragmentation awareness

## Code Example

```c
typedef struct Block {
  struct Block* next;
} Block;

typedef struct Arena {
  Block* blocks;
  size_t count;
} Arena;

static __thread Block* freelist_head = NULL;

void init_arena(Arena* a, void* mem, size_t blocks) {
  a->blocks = (Block*)mem;
  a->count = blocks;
  for (size_t i = 0; i + 1 < blocks; ++i) {
    a->blocks[i].next = &a->blocks[i+1];
  }
  a->blocks[blocks - 1].next = NULL;
  freelist_head = a->blocks;
}

void* tl_alloc() {
  Block* b = freelist_head;
  if (!b) return NULL;
  freelist_head = b->next;
  return (void*)b;
}

void tl_free(void* p) {
  ((Block*)p)->next = freelist_head;
  freelist_head = (Block*)p;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you handle cross-thread freelist sharing to reduce fragmentation and improve memory reuse?
- What ABI- or allocator-related tests would you add to catch use-after-free or double-free bugs?

## Why This Question Is Asked
This question assesses practical memory management skills and understanding of lock-free data structures—essential competencies for systems programming and performance-critical applications.

## Key Concepts
- **Lock-free free-list design**: Using an atomic head pointer enables concurrent allocation/deallocation without locks
- **Memory layout**: Fixed-size 64-byte blocks with the next pointer as the first field ensure proper alignment on 64-bit systems
- **ABA problem**: Tagged pointers (using the unused bits in a 64-bit pointer) can prevent ABA issues in high-contention scenarios
- **Contention testing**: Multi-threaded stress tests verify correctness under concurrent access patterns

## Code Example
```c
typedef struct Block {
    struct Block* next;
    unsigned char payload[56];
} Block;

static _Atomic(Block*) free_head;

Block* allocate(void) {
    Block* head;
    Block* new_head;
    do {
        head = atomic_load_explicit(&free_head, memory_order_acquire);
        if (!head) return NULL;
        new_head = head->next;
    } while (!atomic_compare_exchange_weak_explicit(
        &free_head, &head, new_head,
        memory_order_release, memory_order_relaxed));
    return head;
}

void free_block(void* ptr) {
    Block* block = (Block*)ptr;
    Block* head;
    do {
        head = atomic_load_explicit(&free_head, memory_order_acquire);
        block->next = head;
    } while (!atomic_compare_exchange_weak_explicit(
        &free_head, &head, block,
        memory_order_release, memory_order_relaxed));
}
```

## Test Plan (4 Threads)
1. **Initialization**: Preallocate all N blocks, verify free_head points to the full list
2. **Basic correctness**: Single-threaded allocate/free sequences, verify all blocks returned
3. **Concurrent stress**: 4 threads repeatedly allocate and free blocks; track allocation counts to ensure no leaks or double-frees
4. **Boundary conditions**: Allocate until empty, verify NULL returned; free all blocks, verify full recovery
5. **Data integrity**: Write patterns to payload, free, allocate fresh blocks, verify previous data is not present

