- What trade-offs arise if you instead make it a set-associative cache?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests understanding of basic memory management without dynamic allocation, a common pattern in low-latency systems.

## Key Concepts

- Fixed-size pool basics
- Free-list within block memory
- Alignment and safety checks
- Simple double-free detection (debug)

## Code Example

```c
#define BLOCK_SIZE 64
#define N_BLOCKS 1024
typedef struct Block { struct Block* next; unsigned char payload[BLOCK_SIZE - sizeof(struct Block*)]; } Block;
static Block blocks[N_BLOCKS];
static Block* free_list;

void pool_init(void){
  for(int i=0;i<N_BLOCKS-1;i++) blocks[i].next = &blocks[i+1];
  blocks[N_BLOCKS-1].next = NULL;
  free_list = &blocks[0];
}
void* pool_alloc(void){
  if(!free_list) return NULL;
  Block* b = free_list;
  free_list = b->next;
  return (void*)b->payload;
}
void pool_free(void* p){
  Block* b = (Block*)((char*)p - offsetof(Block, payload));
  b->next = free_list;
  free_list = b;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you detect double-free in a release build?
- How would you extend this to multi-threaded use without locks?

## Why This Is Asked

Assess understanding of low-level synchronization, atomic operations, and memory ordering in a practical, portable way.

## Key Concepts

- Spinlock using atomic_flag
- Memory ordering: acquire on lock, release on unlock
- Exponential backoff with PAUSE to reduce contention
- Correctness test with concurrent increments

## Code Example

```c
#include <stdatomic.h>
#include <immintrin.h>

typedef struct { atomic_flag flag; } spinlock;

static inline void spinlock_init(spinlock* s) {
  atomic_flag_clear(&s->flag);
}

static inline void spin_lock(spinlock* s) {
  while (atomic_flag_test_and_set_explicit(&s->flag, memory_order_acquire)) {
    _mm_pause();
    // small exponential backoff
    for (int b = 1; b < 1024; b <<= 1) {
      _mm_pause();
    }
  }
}

static inline void spin_unlock(spinlock* s) {
  atomic_flag_clear_explicit(&s->flag, memory_order_release);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you adapt this for NUMA systems to reduce cross-socket traffic?
- What are the trade-offs of using a ticket-lock vs this spinlock in high-contention workloads?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests understanding of low-level synchronization primitives, cache-line considerations, and safe reuse of barriers in multi-threaded code.

## Key Concepts

- Sense-reversing barrier pattern
- Cache-line padding to avoid false sharing
- Atomic operations and memory_order semantics

## Code Example

```c
// barrier_init
void barrier_init(barrier_t *b, int n) { b->n = n; b->count = 0; b->sense = false; }

// barrier_wait
void barrier_wait(barrier_t *b, bool *local) {
    bool local_sense = !*local;
    if (atomic_fetch_add(&b->count, 1) == b->n - 1) {
        atomic_store(&b->count, 0);
        atomic_store(&b->sense, local_sense);
    } else {
        while (atomic_load(&b->sense) != local_sense) pause();
    }
    *local = local_sense;
}
```

